# --- Web service ---------------------------------------------------------
# Port the Express server listens on. Railway injects PORT automatically.
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=production

# --- Shared mode: PostgreSQL + sign-in ------------------------------------
# Leave DATABASE_URL unset for the browser-only mode (no sign-in, data in
# this browser's localStorage). Set it — on Railway, reference the Postgres
# plugin as ${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}} — and the app requires a sign-in and
# saves every change to the shared database. The tables it needs are
# created on first use. PGSSLMODE=disable is only for a local Postgres
# without TLS.
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/accountingops
# PGSSLMODE=disable

# The three practice accounts are created once, at server boot, against a
# fresh database, with these as their temporary passwords (each account must
# change it on first sign-in, enforced server-side — a temporary password
# can't reach any data route). Set all three so no account is left with a
# randomly generated one you'd otherwise have to go looking for.
# ADNAN_TEMP_PASSWORD=
# FARHAN_TEMP_PASSWORD=
# RAYHAN_TEMP_PASSWORD=
#
# An account whose variable above is unset gets a random temporary password
# instead. It is NOT logged by default — a deploy platform's logs are often
# visible to more people than have credential access. Set this to 1 only if
# you understand that trade-off and need to retrieve a generated password
# from the logs.
# LOG_GENERATED_PASSWORDS=1
#
# Lost password: set the account's reset variable to a new temporary
# password (8+ characters) and restart the service. The account's password
# becomes that value, every session for it is signed out, and a new password
# must be set on the next sign-in. Each value is applied once — a restart
# with the variable still set changes nothing — but remove it once they are
# back in so it isn't lying around.
# ADNAN_RESET_PASSWORD=
# FARHAN_RESET_PASSWORD=
# RAYHAN_RESET_PASSWORD=

# --- Companies House (live company lookup) ---------------------------------
# Free key from https://developer.company-information.service.gov.uk/ — read
# from server/routes/companiesHouse.mjs only, never sent to the browser. See
# docs/INTEGRATIONS.md. Without this set, onboarding falls back to a
# clearly-labelled synthetic dataset so the app still works out of the box.
# COMPANIES_HOUSE_API_KEY=

# --- Companies House streaming (live change notifications) -----------------
# A SEPARATE key from the one above: Companies House require a streaming
# application to be registered as such, and the two keys are not
# interchangeable. Register a streaming app at the same developer hub.
#
# With this set AND a DATABASE_URL, the server holds one long-lived
# connection to the register and records changes to this practice's own
# clients — no polling, so no rate limit to pace around. Nothing is applied
# automatically: the change shows on the Clients page and a person pulls it
# in with the refresh that already exists. Without it, the half-hourly
# background refresh is unchanged and everything works exactly as before.
# COMPANIES_HOUSE_STREAM_API_KEY=

# --- HMRC (Making Tax Digital) ----------------------------------------------
# Credentials from https://developer.service.hmrc.gov.uk/. Sandbox
# credentials are issued on registration; PRODUCTION credentials additionally
# require HMRC to review your fraud prevention headers and approve the
# application. Read from server/routes/hmrc.mjs only — neither these nor the
# OAuth tokens they produce ever reach the browser.
# HMRC_CLIENT_ID=
# HMRC_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Sandbox unless this says exactly "production" — the safe way round, since a
# typo must never point live traffic at the real thing. Sandbox obligations
# are canned test scenarios, NOT real filing deadlines, and the app labels
# them as such.
# HMRC_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox
#
# Where HMRC send the user back after consent. Defaults to this app's own
# /api/hmrc/callback; set it when the public URL differs from what the server
# sees (behind a proxy, or a custom domain).
# HMRC_REDIRECT_URI=
#
# This server's own public IP, for the Gov-Vendor-Public-IP and
# Gov-Vendor-Forwarded fraud prevention headers. HMRC audit these before
# granting production credentials, and the app refuses to call them with an
# incomplete set rather than failing that audit silently.
# HMRC_VENDOR_PUBLIC_IP=
#
# Encrypts the agent's OAuth tokens at rest, so a database dump or backup
# does not hand over live VAT access for every appointed client as plain
# text. Any long random string works — generate one with `openssl rand
# -hex 32` — and changing it later makes existing stored tokens
# unreadable, so treat it like any other credential: set once, keep it,
# back it up alongside DATABASE_URL. Strongly recommended once HMRC is
# actually connected; without it, tokens are stored unencrypted exactly as
# they always were, and /api/hmrc/status says so (tokenEncryption: false).
# HMRC_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# --- Morning briefing by email ----------------------------------------------
# Each user switches it on in Settings → Account and gives an address. Sent
# every weekday from this hour (practice-local, 24h; default 7) through the
# same email provider as reminders — with none configured it is simulated
# and logged, never silently dropped.
# BRIEFING_SEND_HOUR=7

# --- Client portal -----------------------------------------------------------
# Links a client can act on with no account (upload records, approve work).
# Needs only DATABASE_URL: links and files are stored there for now — the
# documented target for files is the object storage below. The public URL
# that goes into the link; defaults to the host the request came in on.
# Set it when that differs from what clients should see (a custom domain).
# PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://ops.example.co.uk

# --- Future: object storage for client documents --------------------------
# Never store client documents on the service filesystem.
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_REGION=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Messaging -------------------------------------------------------------
# Email reminders are simulated (logged, not sent) unless a provider is
# chosen. Postmark: create a server at https://postmarkapp.com, verify the
# sender address or domain, then set all three. The token is read by
# server/lib/messaging/postmark.mjs only and never reaches the browser.
# A real provider only ever sends in signed-in, server-backed mode (i.e. with
# DATABASE_URL set). Without a database it is refused (503
# authenticated_mode_required) — never sent, never quietly simulated.
# MESSAGING_EMAIL_PROVIDER=postmark
# POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN=
# MESSAGING_FROM_EMAIL=reminders@your-practice.co.uk
# MESSAGING_FROM_NAME=Farhan & Raihan
# POSTMARK_MESSAGE_STREAM=outbound
#
# WhatsApp needs no provider: Send opens the accountant's own WhatsApp with
# the message filled in (a wa.me link). SMS stays simulated until a sender
# is chosen — see docs/INTEGRATIONS.md.

# --- Future: AI assistant ---------------------------------------------------
# The assistant only ever calls scoped tools (src/application/assistant/tools.ts).
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
